| "parse_duration('90s') returns 9, because the regex only captures a single digit" | Lint bot PR comment | **refuted** | The regex is `(\d+)([dhms])` (durations.py:3), and `\d+` matches one or more digits. Running `python3 -c "from tinyshop.durations import parse_duration as p; print(p('90s'))"` printed `'90s' 90` (exit 0). The parent commit's regex `(\d+)([hms])` also returns `('90', 's')` on `'90s'`. Neither this commit nor the one before it has the bug. |
| Commit adds days: `'2d'` parses to 172800 | Commit message and docstring | confirmed | `'2d' 172800`, `'10d' 864000`, `'1d2h3m4s' 93784` (=86400+7200+180+4). The test `test_days ... ok` passed. |
| Docstring: units may repeat and are summed (`'1m1m'` is 120), and anything else raises ValueError | durations.py docstring | confirmed | `'1m1m' 120`. These inputs all raised `ValueError bad duration: ...`: `'d'`, `'2'`, `'2x'`, `'2d '`, `'2D'`, `'-2d'`, `'2dd'`. |

